<h2>An unusual statute in a familiar setting</h2>
<p>The backdrop is a matrimonial conflict of long standing: a 2012 marriage that lasted weeks, a divorce granted by a Family Court in 2020 with the appeal pending, and years of cross-litigation. What distinguishes the case is the statute invoked. Rather than the usual matrimonial provisions, the complainant — who has 86% permanent locomotor disability — filed a complaint under Section 92 of the RPwD Act, 2016, alleging insults and humiliation directed at his disability, from the period of the marriage through to incidents in 2025, including interference at his workplace.</p>

<h2>The threshold error</h2>
<p>The Special Court\'s dismissal proceeded on three grounds: vagueness, staleness and abuse of process. Each is a legitimate consideration — but only after the whole complaint is read. The High Court\'s criticism was precise: the order overlooked the alleged continuing conduct, most notably the incident of 5 July 2025, and judged the complaint as if it concerned 2012 alone. A complaint that alleges a course of conduct cannot be dismissed by testing only its earliest chapter.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Complaint filed (2025)</strong> — alleging disability-based humiliation from 2012 onwards, including recent incidents and workplace interference.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Dismissed at threshold (15.12.2025)</strong> — as vague, stale and an abuse of process, without summoning or pre-summoning evidence.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Revision allowed (13.10.2026)</strong> — order set aside as perfunctory; the complaint must be considered afresh as a whole.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>On remand</strong> — the Special Court may seek further particulars, record pre-summoning evidence, and pass a reasoned order either way.</div>
</div>

<h2>Revision against dismissal of a complaint</h2>
<p>A complainant whose complaint is dismissed at the threshold is not without remedy. The dismissal of a complaint is a final order for the complainant, and the revisional jurisdiction of the Sessions Court or the High Court — under Sections 397/401 CrPC, now Sections 438/442 of the BNSS — permits examination of its legality and propriety. The standard is not whether the revisional court would have summoned the accused, but whether the Magistrate or Special Court applied the correct legal approach. Failing to read the complaint as a whole, ignoring material allegations, or dismissing without the brief reasoned application of mind the law requires are all recognised grounds of interference — and this judgment adds a clear illustration in the RPwD context.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers on RPwD complaints</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Issue</th><th>Guidance from the judgment</th></tr>
<tr><td>Forum</td><td>Complaints under Section 92 go to the designated Special Court for RPwD Act offences in the district</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Set out specific incidents with dates; a course of conduct should be pleaded as such, linking older and recent events</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old allegations</td><td>Historical conduct alone invites a staleness objection; continuing or recent incidents must be clearly particularised</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vagueness objections</td><td>Can be met through further particulars and pre-summoning evidence rather than outright dismissal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parallel matrimonial litigation</td><td>Does not by itself make a disability-rights complaint an abuse of process; the complaint must be tested on its own allegations</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The wider point</h2>
<p>Disability-rights enforcement in India has largely run through the Commissioners and the writ jurisdiction; the criminal provisions of the RPwD Act remain sparsely litigated. This decision does not expand their substance, but it protects their procedural integrity: a Special Court must engage with what is actually alleged before closing the door. For respondents, equally, the judgment preserves every defence — vagueness, limitation, abuse of process — provided the court addresses them against the complaint as a whole, by a reasoned order.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The revision petition was disposed of with observations expressly confined to the legality of the dismissal order; nothing in the judgment, or in this note, bears on the truth of the allegations, which remain to be tested before the Special Court.</p>
</div>
